          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is cognitive behavioural therapy described as first-line for insomnia rather than medication?",
          "answer": "Cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT) for insomnia is described as first-line for chronic insomnia in adults. The source is the sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance, titled \"Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment.\" It gives two main reasons for placing it ahead of medication:\n\n- **Lasting effects:** The benefits of CBT persist after treatment ends. The effects of medication generally do not.\n- **Fewer long-term harms:** CBT avoids the harms linked with long-term use of sleep medicines (hypnotics).\n\nThe same guidance says CBT is delivered as a package over roughly four to eight sessions. It includes stimulus control (using the bed only for sleep, and getting up when unable to sleep) and sleep restriction (limiting time in bed toward actual sleep time, then extending it gradually).\n\nThe retrieved material does not explain the underlying mechanism in detail, compare the size of the benefit against each medication, or cover when medication might still be considered. Those questions are worth raising with a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
